From fed760376ec1572386ac94c0743be0dfdf0d10ba Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jason Zaugg <jzaugg@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 08:06:41 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] Merkle: compose with upstream ancestors reached through an
 object

A parent reached through an object, `class C extends Inst.B` with
`object Inst extends Outer` and B a member class of Outer, is stored as
`p.Inst.B`, which no class is called. The descendant rules in C's own
subproject find B through the external inheritance relation, but the
hashes composed for another subproject took C's ancestors only from its
stored linearization, so they left out B and the A above it. With C
abstract in a middle subproject and `class D extends C` downstream,
removing B's implementation of A's `m` changed no hash D's subproject
could see, and D, which no longer implements m, did not recompile.

Composition now also takes the upstream parents of the class and of its
ancestors in its own subproject from the external inheritance relation,
and the ancestors their stored linearizations list, after the stored
ones; names the stored linearization already has are not added again,
so classes without such a parent compose as before. The composed class
lists the added ancestors among its parents, so that the descendant
rules downstream, which read it from that subproject's analysis, find
the deferred member too.

Under pipelining a downstream subproject reads the early analysis,
which kept only the APIs and the product names; under Merkle hashing it
now keeps the external inheritance relation as well.
